WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:05.400
Fans have been desperate for a follow-up&nbsp;
to Quentin Tarantino's seminal film "Pulp&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.400 --> 00:00:08.400
Fiction" since it hit the silver&nbsp;
screen, but thirty years later,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:08.400 --> 00:00:11.280
it seems unlikely that we'll ever see a sequel.

00:00:11.769 --> 00:00:17.040
"Pulp Fiction" was the second feature film made&nbsp;
by writer-director Quentin Tarantino. He's now&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.040 --> 00:00:22.620
closing in on his tenth, and in 2023,&nbsp;
he celebrated his 60th birthday. Sadly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.620 --> 00:00:27.360
Tarantino has often talked up the notion&nbsp;
of retiring after both of those milestones.

00:00:27.360 --> 00:00:32.820
Unfortunately for die-hard "Pulp Fiction"&nbsp;
fans, that tenth film isn't going to be a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.820 --> 00:00:38.460
follow-up to the 1994 classic. Instead, it's&nbsp;
a project called "The Movie Critic," which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.460 --> 00:00:44.940
is about a 1970s critic who reviews films for a&nbsp;
porno magazine. If it really is his final film,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.940 --> 00:00:50.580
that kills off quite a few potential Tarantino&nbsp;
projects. At various points in his career,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.580 --> 00:00:56.040
he's floated ideas like a gangster-style&nbsp;
"Star Trek" movie, a third "Kill Bill" entry,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.040 --> 00:01:01.140
and a movie about Black soldiers during&nbsp;
World War II. So assuming that Tarantino&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.140 --> 00:01:05.820
sticks to his guns when it comes to feature&nbsp;
film retirement, we won't be seeing any of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.820 --> 00:01:11.240
those movies play out on the big screen. In other&nbsp;
words, the time has passed for "Pulp Fiction 2."

00:01:11.240 --> 00:01:16.440
Other persistent issues have long dogged&nbsp;
"Pulp Fiction 2": ownership and rights. As&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.440 --> 00:01:20.940
it turns out, it may have been tough for&nbsp;
Tarantino to even legally make a sequel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.940 --> 00:01:25.140
to his own film. The director owns the&nbsp;
copyright of the "Pulp Fiction" script,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.140 --> 00:01:31.800
but Miramax owns the finished film. Evidently,&nbsp;
when Tarantino tried to make "Pulp Fiction" NFTs,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.800 --> 00:01:37.500
he wound up in a lawsuit with Miramax that&nbsp;
ended in 2022. If just making non-fungible&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.500 --> 00:01:42.300
tokens was that contentious, imagine how it&nbsp;
might've hampered the making of another movie.

00:01:42.900 --> 00:01:48.360
Over the last three decades, "Pulp Fiction"&nbsp;
writer-director Quentin Tarantino has openly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.360 --> 00:01:53.640
discussed at least three films that would've&nbsp;
expanded the film's universe. In the 1990s,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.640 --> 00:01:58.920
following the back-to-back successes of "Reservoir&nbsp;
Dogs" and "Pulp Fiction," Tarantino toyed with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.920 --> 00:02:04.820
the idea of linking the two films in a new&nbsp;
project called "Double V Vega." In that movie,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.820 --> 00:02:09.060
Michael Madsen and John Travolta would've&nbsp;
reprised their respective "Reservoir Dogs"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.060 --> 00:02:14.230
and "Pulp Fiction" roles of Vic Vega and&nbsp;
Vincent Vega — because they're brothers.

00:02:14.230 --> 00:02:18.360
Essentially a prequel to both movies, it&nbsp;
would have been about the Vega siblings&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.360 --> 00:02:23.460
hanging out in Amsterdam, a trip that Vincent&nbsp;
Vega alludes to in his "Pulp Fiction" opening&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.460 --> 00:02:28.080
monologue. Tarantino never went anywhere&nbsp;
with "Double V Vega," which was also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.080 --> 00:02:33.420
billed as "The Vega Brothers," other than that&nbsp;
premise. And given that 30 years have passed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.420 --> 00:02:37.740
it's probably too late for Madsen and Travolta&nbsp;
to play younger versions of their characters.

00:02:38.015 --> 00:02:39.815
As for other possibilities,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.815 --> 00:02:43.140
there's a scene in "Pulp Fiction" in&nbsp;
which Jules Winnfield tells Vincent,

00:02:43.308 --> 00:02:44.988
"I'm just going to walk the Earth."

00:02:44.988 --> 00:02:46.320
"Whatchu you mean 'walk the earth'?"

00:02:46.320 --> 00:02:51.360
“You know, like Caine in 'Kung Fu.' Walk from&nbsp;
place to place, meet people, get in adventures."

00:02:51.360 --> 00:02:56.820
Tarantino thought about making that single line&nbsp;
of dialog into a whole movie, but he told Movie&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.820 --> 00:03:02.400
Nerds that it never moved beyond the idea realm.&nbsp;
As for the third potential film, while promoting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.400 --> 00:03:09.000
"Kill Bill" in 2003, Tarantino told Aotearoa&nbsp;
News that a sequel was stirring. He said,

00:03:09.000 --> 00:03:11.700
"There's an idea I have. Bottom line,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.700 --> 00:03:17.100
the studio wants it, the fans want it. I'm&nbsp;
sure I can compromise somewhere. It'd be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.100 --> 00:03:21.060
my way of apologizing for never getting&nbsp;
'Vega Brothers' off the ground, I guess."

00:03:21.243 --> 00:03:24.903
Nevertheless, that movie never did get produced.

00:03:24.903 --> 00:03:29.520
Due to the events of the first "Pulp Fiction"&nbsp;
and circumstances affecting the cast's ability&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.520 --> 00:03:34.260
or willingness to participate in the&nbsp;
present day, any follow-up film set in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.260 --> 00:03:39.660
the same cinematic universe couldn't serve as&nbsp;
a direct sequel. For example, Vincent Vega,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.660 --> 00:03:45.120
portrayed by John Travolta in the film, doesn't&nbsp;
make it out of "Pulp Fiction" alive, while Bruce&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.120 --> 00:03:50.940
Willis, who played rogue boxer Butch Coolidge,&nbsp;
retired from acting after developing dementia.

00:03:50.940 --> 00:03:55.980
As for the rest of the cast, it seems like&nbsp;
it might take Tarantino a lot of work to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.980 --> 00:04:01.800
convince Samuel L. Jackson to reprise his&nbsp;
role of hitman Jules Winnfield. In 2022,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.800 --> 00:04:06.600
Tarantino derisively discussed the&nbsp;
"Marvel-ization of Hollywood" on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.600 --> 00:04:12.540
podcast "2 Bears, 1 Cave." Jackson portrays Nick&nbsp;
Fury throughout the Marvel Cinematic Universe and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.540 --> 00:04:17.465
took umbrage at his "Pulp Fiction" director's&nbsp;
comments, firing back at him on "The View."

00:04:17.465 --> 00:04:22.560
As for Uma Thurman, who played Mia Wallace,&nbsp;
her relationship with Tarantino has been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.560 --> 00:04:28.620
rocky. In 2018, she told The New York Times&nbsp;
that she nearly died in a car stunt gone awry&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.620 --> 00:04:32.460
during the filming of "Kill Bill," a&nbsp;
stunt the director reportedly forced&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.460 --> 00:04:37.860
her to do. And allegedly, Tarantino spat on&nbsp;
her and choked her for two separate scenes.

00:04:38.089 --> 00:04:43.369
Thurman has said that she forgave Tarantino for&nbsp;
the car incident after the director apologized.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.369 --> 00:04:47.220
And it's very likely the filmmaker could&nbsp;
patch things up with Jackson over their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.220 --> 00:04:51.360
Marvel feud. Still, it seems like there&nbsp;
might be some friction between Tarantino&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.360 --> 00:04:55.620
and the key players involved. So even if&nbsp;
a "Pulp Fiction 2" were a possibility,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:55.620 --> 00:04:58.980
getting the band back together might&nbsp;
be pretty difficult at this point.

00:04:59.580 --> 00:05:04.920
Writing, directing, producing, and releasing a&nbsp;
movie, particularly a sequel to a 30-year-old&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.920 --> 00:05:10.260
film, is a difficult and complicated task. It&nbsp;
might be more pragmatic and easier for Tarantino&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.260 --> 00:05:15.180
to release the long-rumored and often-speculated&nbsp;
"Pulp Fiction" follow-up in another format,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.180 --> 00:05:19.380
like the written word, a medium Tarantino&nbsp;
has actively explored in recent years.

00:05:19.655 --> 00:05:25.740
In 2021, he published "Once Upon a Time in&nbsp;
Hollywood," a novelization and expansion of his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:25.740 --> 00:05:32.100
2019 film of the same name. And according to Dave&nbsp;
Poland, in the 2010s, Tarantino began writing a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:32.100 --> 00:05:37.800
literary spinoff of his "Django Unchained" project&nbsp;
called "Django in White Hell," which evolved into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:37.800 --> 00:05:42.960
"The Hateful Eight." However, he did give Django&nbsp;
life on the page, pairing the gunfighting hero&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:42.960 --> 00:05:48.480
up with one of the world's most famous masked&nbsp;
vigilantes in the comic book "Django/Zorro."

00:05:48.480 --> 00:05:52.860
And while these aren't real sequels, other&nbsp;
artists and professionals have revisited&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.860 --> 00:05:56.315
the world of "Pulp Fiction"&nbsp;
in their projects. In 2023,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:56.315 --> 00:06:01.560
Deadline reported that production had started&nbsp;
on "Stealing Pulp Fiction," a heist movie from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:01.560 --> 00:06:06.360
director-writer Danny Turkiewicz about a&nbsp;
group of friends who try to abscond with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.360 --> 00:06:12.060
Tarantino's copy of the original 35-millimeter&nbsp;
"Pulp Fiction" film reels. And back in 2014,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:12.060 --> 00:06:17.040
Harvey Keitel reprised his role as the&nbsp;
inscrutable and effective "Pulp Fiction"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:17.040 --> 00:06:22.740
fixer Winston Wolfe in a series of commercials&nbsp;
for U.K. insurance company Direct Line.

